E22 asked whether the dual-parameter attention architecture could reproduce graded familiarity intrinsically, without the external bank mechanism used in the earlier line. The architecture gave each hidden layer a standard semantic parameter set trained by gradient descent and a separate familiarity parameter set consisting of detached prototype buffers updated only by experience. Familiarity was read out with nearest-prototype max cosine certainty and tested on the same four-level frequency design from E19: core, familiar, rare, and novel.

The result is a conditional pass with a documented resolution limit. Jonckheere-Terpstra was significant in 5 of 5 seeds for both layers, and mean Spearman rho was -0.739 for layer 1 and -0.740 for layer 2, confirming a strong monotonic familiarity trend. The architecture correctly ordered core > familiar > rare in all seeds. The failure point was specifically the rare-novel boundary. Strict mean ordering held in 3 of 5 seeds for layer 1 and 1 of 5 seeds for layer 2 because the model did not reliably separate rare combinations seen 2 times from novel combinations seen 0 times.

The gap statistics show where the architecture is working and where it reaches its resolution limit. For layer 1, the core-novel gap across seeds was 0.209, 0.149, 0.196, 0.208, and 0.183, averaging about 0.189. The familiar-rare gap was 0.125, 0.088, 0.099, 0.072, and 0.099, averaging about 0.097. The rare-novel gap was 0.026, -0.008, -0.014, 0.009, and 0.002, averaging about 0.003. For layer 2, the core-novel gap was 0.102, 0.096, 0.094, 0.127, and 0.114, averaging about 0.107. The familiar-rare gap was 0.090, 0.116, 0.079, 0.052, and 0.109, averaging about 0.089. The rare-novel gap was -0.030, -0.048, -0.014, 0.012, and -0.053, averaging about -0.029. The full-range and within-trained-level gaps are large, positive, and consistent. The rare-novel gap is small and noisy.

The resolution limit has a clear cause. With K_p = 32 prototypes and lambda = 0.99 EMA decay, two rare exposures move a prototype by only about 2 percent of the distance to the activation. That is 2 / (1 / (1 - lambda)) = 2 / 100 = 2 percent movement. At that scale, a rare combination is not displaced far enough from novel combinations in prototype space to support reliable rare-novel separation. The architecture is therefore working correctly at the scale it can resolve, while the rare-novel inversion marks the current prototype-resolution limit rather than a fundamental failure of the dual-parameter design. Higher prototype capacity, faster EMA, or more rare exposures should resolve it.

The secondary findings support the architecture. E22 converged in the same or fewer epochs than baseline in all 5 seeds and reached the same final training accuracy in all 5 seeds. Mean stopping epoch was 78.8 for E22 versus 84.4 for baseline. Novel test accuracy was higher for E22 in 4 of 5 seeds, with mean novel accuracy 0.630 versus 0.583 for baseline.

The interpretation boundary is that E22 validates the architectural direction. The dual-parameter attention layer produces an intrinsic graded familiarity signal with strong monotonic structure and no external bank. What remains unresolved is the fine rare-versus-novel boundary under the current prototype resolution. The next experiments should treat that as an explicit capacity and update-scale question, not as evidence that the architecture itself is absent.
